the first thing which perplexes me is the idiocy of sleep.

66
00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.900
When you think of sleep an evolutionary perspective,

67
00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.170
firstly, you're not finding a mate, you're not reproducing.

68
00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.130
You're not caring for your young.

69
00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:09.700
You're not foraging for food and worst of all,

70
00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.120
your vulnerable to predation.

71
00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:12.953
So--

72
00:03:12.953 --> 00:03:14.950
Not only that, you're an insane person.

73
00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:15.983
You go through this whole thing,

74
00:03:15.983 --> 00:03:18.880
like if you were awake and you were experiencing the states

75
00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.480
that you experienced when you're asleep,

76
00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.460
you would be institutionalized.

77
00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:23.667
That's right. Yeah.

78
00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:24.760
And that's what we call dream sleep,

79
00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:26.450
which is, seems to be normal.

80
00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:29.010
But you know, on any one of those grounds,

81
00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.400
sleep should have been strongly selected against

82
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.428
in the course of evolution.

83
00:03:34.428 --> 00:03:38.490
But the fact that sleep has fought its way through,

84
00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.940
along every step of the evolutionary pathway.

85
00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:42.160
In fact, if we look back,

86
00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.300
we can even find sleep like states in earthworms,

87
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:49.120
which are ancient from an evolutionary perspective.

88
00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:49.953
So in other words,

89
00:03:49.953 --> 00:03:53.770
sleep evolved with life itself on this planet.

90
00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:56.720
And then despite all of those trappings,

91
00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.870
it fought its way through heroically,

92
00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:02.220
every step along the evolutionary pathway.

93
00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.650
In other words, if sleep doesn't serve

94
00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.280
some absolutely vital set of functions,

95
00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:08.730
it's probably the biggest mistake

96
00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.660
that the evolutionary processes has ever made.

97
00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:12.630
And we've now realized

98
00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.430
that it didn't make a spectacular (indistinct)

99
00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:20.260
but that premise, that sleep evolved is usually founded

100
00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.850
on the idea that we were awake.

101
00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:25.340
And then we evolved to sleep.

102
00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:26.977
And one thought that crossed my mind

103
00:04:26.977 --> 00:04:30.630
and I was perhaps stupid enough to write it in the book was,

104
00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.100
I wonder if it's the other way around,

105
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:36.780
why don't we assume that we evolved sleep?

106
00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:41.170
Why don't we assume that sleep is the defacto state

107
00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.910
of all birth life on planet earth?

108
00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:49.910
And it's from sleep that wakefulness emerged and evolved.

109
00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:53.540
Now, shy of a time capsule or some serious smelling salts,

110
00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:55.020
I'm probably not gonna be able to go back

111
00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.253
and figure that out.

112
00:04:57.253 --> 00:04:59.640
But I think it's an interesting sort of way to-

113
00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.900
Well, the evolutionary advantage of wakefulness

114
00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:03.930
is self-evident.

115
00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:06.490
We need to procreate, we need to feed ourselves.

116
00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:10.990
But less obvious is the evolutionary advantage of sleep.

117
00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:14.300
It just strikes us as being something that gets in the way

118
00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.870
as opposed to crucial for all the functions

119
00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:19.380
that you kind of go through (indistinct) in the book.

120
00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:22.959
And I think that's perhaps one of the reasons that people

121
00:05:22.959 --> 00:05:26.190
are so misunderstanding of sleep

122
00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:30.950
and so understandably neglecting of sleep,

123
00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:33.990
because most people, if you didn't understand what sleep is,

124
00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:35.520
you would think, well,

125
00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.660
my body gets some rest and my mind is dormant

126
00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.820
and nothing could be further from the truth.

127
00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:47.070
There is an immense cascade of health benefits.

128
00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:50.470
This constellation of night time gifts

129
00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:52.930
that sleep will provide to your body.

130
00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:55.840
And every operation of the mind that we know

131
00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.430
is overhauled some spectacular things.

132
00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:00.670
In fact, during certain stages of sleep,

133
00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:05.670
your brain is up to 30% more active than when you're awake.

134
00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:10.256
And so it's so understanding that we would think, well,

135
00:06:10.256 --> 00:06:11.650
you know, if I didn't know anything about sleep,

136
00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:15.657
surely I can just shave off an hour here or two.

137
00:06:15.657 --> 00:06:18.220
And it just doesn't work like that.

138
00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:20.720
You know, one hour we can think of

139
00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.870
this incredible global experiment

140
00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:27.870
that's performed on well over 60 billion people

141
00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:29.660
across seven to sorry,

142
00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:33.980
well over 2 billion people across 70 countries twice a year,

143
00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:36.430
it's called daylight savings time.

144
00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:39.150
And what we've discovered is that in the Spring,

145
00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.910
when we lose just one hour of sleep,

146
00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:46.870
there is a 24% increase in heart attacks the following day.

147
00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:47.740
So crazy.

148
00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.090
Yet in the Fall-
and then the reverse

149
00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:50.420
in the Fall.
Yeah, exactly.

150
00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:54.320
You sort of see, I think it's a 21% reduction

151
00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.630
in heart attacks, so that's how fragile.

152
00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:59.260
And yet if everybody was sleeping eight hours a night,

153
00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.220
it would be irrelevant.

154
00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.190
So it's less about daylight savings time

155
00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:05.050
than it is about our social constructs

156
00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.310
around when we need to get up and go to work

157
00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:08.320
or go to school.

158
00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:09.153
That's, right.

159
00:07:09.153 --> 00:07:12.053
Yeah. I think, you know, society,

160
00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:18.520
has firstly stigmatized sleep with this label of laziness

161
00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:20.340
that, you know, if you're getting sufficient sleep,

162
00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.270
your maybe slothful.

163
00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:24.980
And sometimes when I'm at speaking events,

164
00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:26.670
people will come up and they'll usually

165
00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.370
wait right until the end of the line of questions.

166
00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:30.570
And they'll whisper sort of to me,

167
00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:33.130
look, I'm one of those people who needs, gosh,

168
00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:35.180
probably about eight hours of sleep.

169
00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:38.160
And you know, as it, this, the worst secret,

170
00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:41.230
the worst than a shameful thing that you could ever suggest.

171
00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:42.670
So I think that's the first issue,

172
00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:47.670
but then we're working long hours and pre-pandemic,

173
00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:52.740
you know, commuting times had also increased.

174
00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:54.520
And when you think about that,

175
00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:56.750
so we're leaving the house earlier.

176
00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:58.810
We're arriving home later,

177
00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:02.520
nobody wants to short change on time with family off

178
00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:04.610
Netflix or whatever it is.

179
00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:08.580
And so the one thing that gets squeezed like vice grips

180
00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:12.110
in the middle of the night is this thing called

181
00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:13.263
a full night of sleep.

182
00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:18.110
Yeah, you're almost some kind of punk rock revolutionary.

183
00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:19.810
You protect your sleep time, right?

184
00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:23.220
It is the one thing that's negotiable or dispensable

185
00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:25.320
when you're looking at how you allocate your time.

186
00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:26.730
That's right, it seems to be.

187
00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:30.930
And the, of course the reality is that sleep alas

188
00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:34.130
is not an optional lifestyle luxury,

189
00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:37.380
it's a non-negotiable biological necessity.

190
00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.980
It is your life support system.

191
00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:42.310
We all know that the people who say

192
00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:45.510
I only need four hours or I only I'm fine on five hours.

193
00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:46.610
And I think you have,

194
00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:50.430
I've heard you say something along the lines of 0% of people

195
00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:52.990
need less than X number of hours.

196
00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:56.640
Like that's all a story people tell themselves.

197
00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.000
But it's simply not the case.

198
00:08:59.000 --> 00:08:59.833
That's right. Yeah.

199
00:08:59.833 --> 00:09:02.390
So I think if you look at the weight of the evidence,

200
00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:06.480
the number of people who can survive on five hours of sleep

201
00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.850
or less without showing any impairment

202
00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:10.240
rounded to a whole number

203
00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.123
and expressed as a percent of the population is zero.

204
00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:19.470
And it's, to me, you would ask the question,

205
00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:22.810
well, why do people think that they're doing okay?

206
00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:27.150
And what we've discovered is that your subjective sense

207
00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:30.400
of how well you're doing when you are insufficiently slept

208
00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:33.170
is a miserable predictor of objectively,

209
00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:35.987
how you're doing when you're insufficiently slept.

210
00:09:35.987 --> 00:09:40.010
And so, it's a little bit like a drunk driver to bar,

211
00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:42.010
they've had six or seven shots

212
00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:44.200
and they pick up their car keys and they say, look,

213
00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:45.830
I'm fine to drive home.

214
00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:47.020
And your response is,

215
00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:49.150
I know that you think you'll find to drive home,

216
00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:52.350
but trust me objectively, you're in no state.

217
00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:54.737
And it's the same way with a lack of sleep.

218
00:09:54.737 --> 00:09:56.083
So that's one of the,

219
00:09:56.083 --> 00:10:00.512
the sort of the flattening aspects of insufficient sleep

220
00:10:00.512 --> 00:10:01.345
that you think you're doing just fine.

221
00:10:01.345 --> 00:10:03.510
And gradually over time,

222
00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:08.340
that deficient form of you becomes the new norm.

223
00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:11.060
And most people don't go from sleeping eight hours

224
00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:13.100
down to five hours.

225
00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:17.250
It's this sort of gradual chipping away at the sleep time.

226
00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:20.770
And they don't realize the version of themselves.

227
00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:22.920
And a great example of this clinically

228
00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:25.703
is in sleep disorders, such as sleep apnea.

229
00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:28.310
And these patients will say to you,

230
00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:32.340
look doc, I think I'm fine, I think I'm doing pretty well.

231
00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:34.950
And then you treat the sleep apnea

232
00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:37.410
so they start sleeping better than not snoring.

233
00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:39.540
They're not gasping for breath.

234
00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:43.060
And within a few weeks they cannot believe

235
00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.780
the transformed state of themselves.

236
00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.940
And I had one patient who said, you know,

237
00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:52.460
it was as though with the sleep apnea device,

238
00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:56.100
I'd come along and I'd wiped a frosted window clear

239
00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:58.000
and finally I could see again.

240
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:01.070
And that's the type of mental benefit that you get

241
00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:03.010
and of course physiologically, it's amazing.

242
00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:04.100
Yeah, yeah.

243
00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:05.433
I mean, I just know, you know,

244
00:11:05.433 --> 00:11:08.660
when you're younger sleep comes easy, as you start to age,

245
00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:11.170
it becomes a little bit more fragile and elusive.

246
00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:14.640
And I found myself having to really be much more intentional

247
00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:15.480
about it

248
00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.640
and the difference between having a good night's sleep

249
00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:19.370
where I get my eight hours

250
00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:23.280
and I know that I've been in a sufficient amount of,

251
00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:27.890
of REM state and REM deep state all of that,

252
00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:32.050
my whole experience of my life is completely different

253
00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:34.560
from those nights where I struggle and wake up

254
00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:37.670
and had maybe six and just not so good,

255
00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:41.690
like everything from, mentally, emotionally,

256
00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:43.950
physically product productivity wise,

257
00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:44.810
like every single,

258
00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:47.860
there is nothing that is not positively impacted

259
00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:49.870
by having a really good night of sleep.

260
00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:51.780
And yet it's for me,

261
00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:52.670
