It's almost like the informational inbox of your brain.

1291
00:58:47.620 --> 00:58:50.410
It's very good at receiving new information and memories

1292
00:58:50.410 --> 00:58:52.280
and holding onto them.

1293
00:58:52.280 --> 00:58:55.630
And that part of the brain had actually deteriorated

1294
00:58:55.630 --> 00:58:58.380
because of that disrupted sleep schedule.

1295
00:58:58.380 --> 00:58:59.750
Now you could argue,

1296
00:58:59.750 --> 00:59:04.113
well, perhaps it's just something about bias selection that,

1297
00:59:05.966 --> 00:59:07.030
I don't know how it would be,

1298
00:59:07.030 --> 00:59:09.240
but airline pilots they just--
(indistinct) Become pilots,

1299
00:59:09.240 --> 00:59:10.770
yeah, because of poor memory

1300
00:59:10.770 --> 00:59:12.460
they don't go into profession X,

1301
00:59:12.460 --> 00:59:15.310
they go into, now piloting is essential with memory.

1302
00:59:15.310 --> 00:59:16.797
And so I don't think that's the case,

1303
00:59:16.797 --> 00:59:18.440
but a better demonstration of that,

1304
00:59:18.440 --> 00:59:19.273
was then they looked at

1305
00:59:19.273 --> 00:59:21.220
how long that they'd been on the job.

1306
00:59:21.220 --> 00:59:25.170
How many years had they been going through that,

1307
00:59:25.170 --> 00:59:27.460
that sort of sleep deficiency.

1308
00:59:27.460 --> 00:59:30.960
And that predicted the magnitude of brain shrinkage,

1309
00:59:30.960 --> 00:59:32.340
which made me think, okay,

1310
00:59:32.340 --> 00:59:33.460
I think it's more likely

1311
00:59:33.460 --> 00:59:35.830
that it has to do with the sleep as well.

1312
00:59:35.830 --> 00:59:40.830
So is the a potential long-term organic atrophy consequence

1313
00:59:41.070 --> 00:59:43.220
of insufficient sleep on the brain.

1314
00:59:43.220 --> 00:59:45.790
We seem to think that there is yes.

1315
00:59:45.790 --> 00:59:48.950
But again, I think I want to be really careful here

1316
00:59:48.950 --> 00:59:52.470
and I don't want to be causing people alarm.

1317
00:59:52.470 --> 00:59:55.230
And what I would say is that it is never too late

1318
00:59:55.230 --> 00:59:56.850
to start sleeping better.

1319
00:59:56.850 --> 00:59:59.270
And that's not just me sort of pulling out the pom-poms

1320
00:59:59.270 --> 01:00:01.660
and trying to be positive. We've got data.

1321
01:00:01.660 --> 01:00:04.830
So in a series of studies and we've done a lot of work in,

1322
01:00:04.830 --> 01:00:06.950
as I mentioned in older adults.

1323
01:00:06.950 --> 01:00:08.910
But if you take a group of midlife adults

1324
01:00:08.910 --> 01:00:12.910
who are suffering from untreated sleep apnea, heavy snoring,

1325
01:00:12.910 --> 01:00:15.000
and then you put them on treatment,

1326
01:00:15.000 --> 01:00:17.063
which is called a CPAP machine,

1327
01:00:18.483 --> 01:00:21.380
C-P-A-P the Continuous Positive Airway Pressure.

1328
01:00:21.380 --> 01:00:24.090
And by the way, if anyone's listening to this

1329
01:00:24.090 --> 01:00:26.640
and they think that they suffer from a sleep apnea,

1330
01:00:26.640 --> 01:00:29.700
or they have a partner who does please go and get diagnosed,

1331
01:00:29.700 --> 01:00:31.683
it is a deathly disease untreated.

1332
01:00:32.710 --> 01:00:35.810
And what they found is that about half of those participants

1333
01:00:35.810 --> 01:00:39.630
complied to the treatment and about half didn't

1334
01:00:39.630 --> 01:00:42.250
and they track them over a 10-year period.

1335
01:00:42.250 --> 01:00:44.080
And what they found is that those individuals

1336
01:00:44.080 --> 01:00:45.550
who comply to the treatment

1337
01:00:45.550 --> 01:00:48.600
and who sleep was improved as a consequence,

1338
01:00:48.600 --> 01:00:52.100
they staved off the onslaught of Alzheimer's disease

1339
01:00:52.100 --> 01:00:56.890
and cognitive decline by anywhere between 10 to 15 years,

1340
01:00:56.890 --> 01:00:59.820
relative to those who remained untreated

1341
01:00:59.820 --> 01:01:01.740
or uncompliant with the treatment.

1342
01:01:01.740 --> 01:01:04.850
In other words, even in midlife, there is evidence to say,

1343
01:01:04.850 --> 01:01:06.610
it's never too late to start sleeping.

1344
01:01:06.610 --> 01:01:08.570
But can those brain centers that,

1345
01:01:08.570 --> 01:01:10.870
that have atrophied be regenerated

1346
01:01:10.870 --> 01:01:13.000
or is that a permanent thing?

1347
01:01:13.000 --> 01:01:14.137
We don't know.

1348
01:01:14.137 --> 01:01:17.040
And it depends on what extent of atrophy.

1349
01:01:17.040 --> 01:01:20.890
We do. We used to think maybe 30 years ago

1350
01:01:20.890 --> 01:01:24.440
that the brain didn't produce new brain cells

1351
01:01:24.440 --> 01:01:27.053
once it had matured and once you are an adult,

1352
01:01:27.053 --> 01:01:30.800
that was your sort of smorgasbord of brain cells.

1353
01:01:30.800 --> 01:01:32.850
And as you lost them through time

1354
01:01:32.850 --> 01:01:34.160
you never got them back.
(indistinct) When you drink,

1355
01:01:34.160 --> 01:01:35.740
you kill brain cells, they never come back.

1356
01:01:35.740 --> 01:01:36.630
They never come back.

1357
01:01:36.630 --> 01:01:37.490
Well, that's not true.

1358
01:01:37.490 --> 01:01:40.450
There are in many regions of the brain that is true,

1359
01:01:40.450 --> 01:01:43.720
but there are a couple of regions that it's not true.

1360
01:01:43.720 --> 01:01:46.110
And in fact, these memory centers, the hippocampus,

1361
01:01:46.110 --> 01:01:47.910
that's one of the centers that does actually

1362
01:01:47.910 --> 01:01:50.633
seem to have some degree of regenerative capacity.

1363
01:01:53.103 --> 01:01:57.660
How capable of meeting the demand of brain atrophy

1364
01:01:57.660 --> 01:02:02.660
and damage that that system is of regeneration is unclear.

1365
01:02:02.670 --> 01:02:06.440
What I should note by the way, is that that system of,

1366
01:02:06.440 --> 01:02:08.140
of the creation of new brain cells,

1367
01:02:08.140 --> 01:02:10.455
it's what we call neurogenesis,

1368
01:02:10.455 --> 01:02:12.850
which I think is a great alternative name for a band

1369
01:02:12.850 --> 01:02:15.033
rather than just Genesis, Phil.

1370
01:02:17.710 --> 01:02:20.850
If you deprive of sleep,

1371
01:02:20.850 --> 01:02:25.210
it fails to have the regulation of that neurogenesis.

1372
01:02:25.210 --> 01:02:27.350
You lose the capacity for neurogenesis

1373
01:02:27.350 --> 01:02:28.770
when you are under slept.

1374
01:02:28.770 --> 01:02:32.390
So it becomes this self-fulfilling negative vicious cycle

1375
01:02:32.390 --> 01:02:35.680
of prophecy that if you are not getting sufficient sleep,

1376
01:02:35.680 --> 01:02:39.230
those brain cells start to deteriorate and atrophy.

1377
01:02:39.230 --> 01:02:41.170
And then the one thing that could help you get them back

1378
01:02:41.170 --> 01:02:43.540
is the one thing that you keep depriving yourself of.

1379
01:02:43.540 --> 01:02:46.849
So you lose even the salvation of the,

1380
01:02:46.849 --> 01:02:48.523
the sort of the salvaging, sorry, I should say of that.

1381
01:02:49.740 --> 01:02:51.890
You mentioned pilots traveling

1382
01:02:51.890 --> 01:02:54.130
from time zone to time zone.

1383
01:02:54.130 --> 01:02:58.060
On top of that, I wanna talk about the medical profession.

1384
01:02:58.060 --> 01:03:00.760
'Cause here in, lie, the greatest irony,

1385
01:03:00.760 --> 01:03:03.830
these people who are dispatched with, you know,

1386
01:03:03.830 --> 01:03:05.880
tending to our health,

1387
01:03:05.880 --> 01:03:09.290
have this complete myopic perspective

1388
01:03:09.290 --> 01:03:12.310
when it comes to prioritizing sleep in their own profession.

1389
01:03:12.310 --> 01:03:16.040
And there's this systemic, you know,

1390
01:03:16.040 --> 01:03:19.580
kind of infrastructure set up that prioritizes

1391
01:03:19.580 --> 01:03:20.480
lack of sleep.

1392
01:03:20.480 --> 01:03:24.110
These residents who have to work these 30 hour shifts,

1393
01:03:24.110 --> 01:03:27.460
this legacy of an old tradition that was set in stone

1394
01:03:27.460 --> 01:03:32.120
long ago and continues like unbelievably to, you know,

1395
01:03:32.120 --> 01:03:35.050
be the standard operating procedure today,

1396
01:03:35.050 --> 01:03:36.530
which is unbelievable.

1397
01:03:36.530 --> 01:03:40.000
And I loved how you told the history of this,

1398
01:03:40.000 --> 01:03:41.323
this guy Halstead, right.

1399
01:03:42.830 --> 01:03:44.870
Who basically was a hard back in,

1400
01:03:44.870 --> 01:03:46.470
at the turn of the century and just said,

1401
01:03:46.470 --> 01:03:48.410
this is the way we're gonna do it.

1402
01:03:48.410 --> 01:03:51.707
And today we still do it in the manner

1403
01:03:51.707 --> 01:03:53.960
that he kind of established back then,

1404
01:03:53.960 --> 01:03:56.610
but it turns out he was like a raging cocaine addict.

1405
01:03:56.610 --> 01:03:57.443
That's right. Yeah.

1406
01:03:57.443 --> 01:04:00.420
So William Halsted was the guy who set up the resident,

1407
01:04:00.420 --> 01:04:03.900
the first resident training program in the United States

1408
01:04:03.900 --> 01:04:05.573
at Johns Hopkins University.

1409
01:04:06.460 --> 01:04:09.770
And, you know, it's called a residency for a reason

1410
01:04:09.770 --> 01:04:11.950
because you're going to become a resident.

1411
01:04:11.950 --> 01:04:14.520
You're going to live in the hospital.

1412
01:04:14.520 --> 01:04:19.410
And he was known for being able to go these heroic

1413
01:04:19.410 --> 01:04:21.817
long stretches without needing sleep.

1414
01:04:21.817 --> 01:04:23.060
And people were stunned.

1415
01:04:23.060 --> 01:04:27.063
And he expected his junior residents to match him in that.

1416
01:04:28.020 --> 01:04:30.090
And gradually after his death,

1417
01:04:30.090 --> 01:04:33.280
it emerged the reason why he was able to do that,

1418
01:04:33.280 --> 01:04:35.140
hosted in his early career

1419
01:04:35.140 --> 01:04:40.140
was actually studying the anesthetic properties of cocaine.

1420
01:04:41.980 --> 01:04:46.730
And unfortunately he started to use cocaine himself

1421
01:04:46.730 --> 01:04:51.730
and he became an accidental addict.

1422
01:04:51.770 --> 01:04:56.200
And that was how he was able to just go days straight,

1423
01:04:56.200 --> 01:05:00.000
without seemingly you know, needing sleep.

1424
01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:00.833
So--
Apparently

1425
01:05:00.833 --> 01:05:03.715
set the expectation that everybody else should be able

1426
01:05:03.715 --> 01:05:04.548
to match-

1427
01:05:04.548 --> 01:05:05.381
Should match.
The way that he was.

1428
01:05:05.381 --> 01:05:06.860
To go at the same degree.

1429
01:05:06.860 --> 01:05:09.010
Now, there were times there were stories where,

1430
01:05:09.010 --> 01:05:12.340
people would say, he would in the operating theater,

1431
01:05:12.340 --> 01:05:13.610
he would have to go and take a break

1432
01:05:13.610 --> 01:05:15.660
because he was saying he was not feeling well.

1433
01:05:15.660 --> 01:05:17.690
He was sweating a lot, he'd seemed be cold.

1434
01:05:17.690 --> 01:05:20.282
And he would have to go take a break. And, you know,

1435
01:05:20.282 --> 01:05:21.960
it's 'cause he was detoxing during along that surgery

1436
01:05:21.960 --> 01:05:23.810
so he had to go and administer again.

1437
01:05:24.820 --> 01:05:26.730
And there were even stories.

1438
01:05:26.730 --> 01:05:30.890
So he knew that he was an addict and he sought

1439
01:05:30.890 --> 01:05:35.270
to go into rehab under a different surname.

1440
01:05:35.270 --> 01:05:38.780
And at the time they were treating cocaine addiction

1441
01:05:38.780 --> 01:05:41.730
with morphine and unfortunately what happened

1442
01:05:41.730 --> 01:05:44.620
is that he came out of that rehabilitation program

1443
01:05:44.620 --> 01:05:47.050
with no resolution to his cocaine addiction,

1444
01:05:47.050 --> 01:05:49.060
but now he had a heroin addiction.

1445
01:05:49.060 --> 01:05:52.700
And the story goes that he would have his shirts,

1446
01:05:52.700 --> 01:05:55.010
his white shirts sent away

1447
01:05:55.010 --> 01:05:58.080
to get laundered in places like Paris (indistinct)

1448
01:05:58.080 --> 01:06:00.810
and they would come back both white and starchy,

1449
01:06:00.810 --> 01:06:02.660
but there would also be other, you know,

1450
01:06:02.660 --> 01:06:07.120
white related compounds in there.

1451
01:06:07.120 --> 01:06:10.760
And we've never let go of that (indistinct)

1452
01:06:10.760 --> 01:06:12.250
and I think inane practice,

1453
01:06:12.250 --> 01:06:15.500
and it's not medical residents who are at fault here,

1454
01:06:15.500 --> 01:06:17.310
you know, speak to any of them.

1455
01:06:17.310 --> 01:06:19.250
And none of them will tell you that

1456
01:06:19.250 --> 01:06:20.823
that's what I want to be doing.

1457
01:06:22.226 --> 01:06:24.620
And I've had conversations with a dear friend

1458
01:06:24.620 --> 01:06:27.900
called Pietra Tia, who went through the medical practices,

1459
01:06:27.900 --> 01:06:29.560
wonderful medical doctor.

1460
01:06:29.560 --> 01:06:31.170
And he describes, you know,

1461
01:06:31.170 --> 01:06:34.570
some shocking history with a lack of sleep.

1462
01:06:34.570 --> 01:06:37.430
And the statistics are just damning.

1463
01:06:37.430 --> 01:06:39.880
Firstly, what we know is that medical residents

1464
01:06:39.880 --> 01:06:42.210
who have performed a 30-hour shift

1465
01:06:42.210 --> 01:06:45.690
will make 460% more diagnostic errors

1466
01:06:45.690 --> 01:06:48.070
in the Intensive Care Unit.

1467
01:06:48.070 --> 01:06:49.050
Secondly, we know that,

1468
01:06:49.050 --> 01:06:51.880
if you're going to have elective surgery, let's say,

1469
01:06:51.880 --> 01:06:54.970
and your surgeon has slept less than six hours

1470
01:06:54.970 --> 01:06:56.523
in the previous 24,

1471
01:06:56.523 --> 01:07:01.523
they are 170% more likely to cause a major surgical error

1472
01:07:01.950 --> 01:07:04.480
such as rupturing a blood vessel or you know,

1473
01:07:04.480 --> 01:07:06.513
damaging or puncturing an organ.

1474
01:07:07.430 --> 01:07:10.710
Then the irony is that a medical resident

1475
01:07:10.710 --> 01:07:12.860
who's worked a 30-hour shift,

1476
01:07:12.860 --> 01:07:15.140
when they get back in their car to drive home

1477
01:07:15.140 --> 01:07:16.023
at the end of their shift,

1478
01:07:16.023 --> 01:07:20.600
they're 168% more likely to get into a car crash.

1479
01:07:20.600 --> 01:07:24.860
Now returning to the accident and emergency room

1480
01:07:24.860 --> 01:07:26.110
from which they probably came

1481
01:07:26.110 --> 01:07:30.474
but now as a patient, rather than a doctor.

1482
01:07:30.474 --> 01:07:34.100
And you know, I've tried with many other people

1483
01:07:34.100 --> 01:07:38.420
to lobby the medical institute and/or institution

1484
01:07:39.540 --> 01:07:42.253
and try to understand why is this the case.

1485
01:07:43.180 --> 01:07:45.780
And I think some of it has to do with the old boys network

1486
01:07:45.780 --> 01:07:47.550
that it's almost like a hazing that, you know,

1487
01:07:47.550 --> 01:07:49.623
we went through it, so you have to do it.

1488
01:07:50.840 --> 01:07:53.590
Man up, suit up, boot up, woman up,

1489
01:07:53.590 --> 01:07:57.310
you need to get through this, this is a rites of passage.

1490
01:07:57.310 --> 01:07:59.742
And again, it comes back to that idea

1491
01:07:59.742 --> 01:08:02.970
that thinking that you can fight mother nature and evolution

1492
01:08:02.970 --> 01:08:05.923
is just thick-headed and that's what--

1493
01:08:05.923 --> 01:08:09.920
I mean, I get the idea that you have to develop

1494
01:08:09.920 --> 01:08:13.100
a level of rigor and emotional resiliency

1495
01:08:13.100 --> 01:08:14.690
to handle that kind of job.

1496
01:08:14.690 --> 01:08:17.080
And there is something to be said for, you know,

1497
01:08:17.080 --> 01:08:21.140
putting people in challenging situations to see how they

1498
01:08:21.140 --> 01:08:22.780
function under high stress,

1499
01:08:22.780 --> 01:08:26.430
but the sleep deprivation aspect of it is just ridiculous.

1500
01:08:26.430 --> 01:08:30.580
You know, (mumbles) when you go through medical school,

1501
01:08:30.580 --> 01:08:33.690
you take an oath, which is to do no harm,

1502
01:08:33.690 --> 01:08:35.150
and then you're placed under conditions

1503
01:08:35.150 --> 01:08:37.320
of insufficient sleep that statistically

1504
01:08:37.320 --> 01:08:39.180
will guarantee that you will do more harm

1505
01:08:39.180 --> 01:08:40.820
than if you were sleeping or working at,

1506
01:08:40.820 --> 01:08:43.010
say just a 16-hour shift.

1507
01:08:43.010 --> 01:08:43.883
One of the push,

1508
01:08:44.830 --> 01:08:48.170
I think some of the pushback that you receive

1509
01:08:48.170 --> 01:08:51.310
or I've received is, look my mind's made up,

1510
01:08:51.310 --> 01:08:52.860
don't confuse me with the data.

1511
01:08:54.110 --> 01:08:55.575
And you think, okay, good--

1512
01:08:55.575 --> 01:08:58.100
You're the scientist--
What can I do?

1513
01:08:58.100 --> 01:09:01.570
You know, isn't this self-evident?

1514
01:09:01.570 --> 01:09:03.510
And one of the arguments though that I think

1515
01:09:03.510 --> 01:09:07.350
that has some legitimacy is that of continuity of care,

1516
01:09:07.350 --> 01:09:10.200
that if you are flip-flopping back and forth

1517
01:09:10.200 --> 01:09:12.430
between a resident, every six hours,

1518
01:09:12.430 --> 01:09:14.920
the continuity of the patient care can decrease.

1519
01:09:14.920 --> 01:09:17.530
But then I thought about that argument for a long time

1520
01:09:17.530 --> 01:09:22.490
and then I looked at a number of medical control, sorry,

1521
01:09:22.490 --> 01:09:24.520
the medical systems throughout the world.

1522
01:09:24.520 --> 01:09:27.600
And I asked, how long does it take to train their residents

1523
01:09:27.600 --> 01:09:31.150
and how good is their medical care?

1524
01:09:31.150 --> 01:09:34.650
And what I found is that there are places like New Zealand,

1525
01:09:34.650 --> 01:09:36.330
France, Switzerland,

1526
01:09:36.330 --> 01:09:40.410
they all have their residents working no more than 16 hours.

1527
01:09:40.410 --> 01:09:43.146
And their quality of healthcare

1528
01:09:43.146 --> 01:09:47.380
is actually ranked far better in the worldwide statistics

1529
01:09:47.380 --> 01:09:48.780
than the United States.

1530
01:09:48.780 --> 01:09:51.920
So, you can't tell me that you can't train an individual

1531
01:09:51.920 --> 01:09:56.920
within five years or less, at a reasonable amount of sleep

1532
01:09:57.080 --> 01:10:00.110
and not maintain high quality of medical practice.

1533
01:10:00.110 --> 01:10:02.610
Well, part of the problem tracks back to the fact

1534
01:10:02.610 --> 01:10:06.840
that there's no education on this in medical school, right?

1535
01:10:06.840 --> 01:10:10.050
So these doctors who then become hospital administrators,

1536
01:10:10.050 --> 01:10:12.230
don't have the proper background

1537
01:10:12.230 --> 01:10:14.840
to make a better decision about this.

1538
01:10:14.840 --> 01:10:15.850
That's exactly right.

1539
01:10:15.850 --> 01:10:19.970
So I also, you know, myself and some other researchers,

1540
01:10:19.970 --> 01:10:24.060
you look at the medical curriculum throughout many of these

1541
01:10:24.060 --> 01:10:25.430
first world nations.

1542
01:10:25.430 --> 01:10:28.490
And what you discover is that on average,

1543
01:10:28.490 --> 01:10:31.700
the average medical resident will receive somewhere between

1544
01:10:31.700 --> 01:10:36.440
an hour and a half to two hours of education on sleep.

1545
01:10:36.440 --> 01:10:39.940
Relative to the entire medical program.

1546
01:10:39.940 --> 01:10:42.423
Now that strikes me because,

1547
01:10:44.040 --> 01:10:47.710
that's one-third of their patients lives.

1548
01:10:47.710 --> 01:10:50.650
And that one-third of their life spent asleep

1549
01:10:50.650 --> 01:10:53.230
makes a profound difference about two-thirds

1550
01:10:53.230 --> 01:10:56.120
of waking health life.

1551
01:10:56.120 --> 01:10:59.743
Why aren't we investing more in the education of sleep

1552
01:10:59.743 --> 01:11:01.780
for our medical residents?

1553
01:11:01.780 --> 01:11:03.383
Well, why aren't we?

1554
01:11:03.383 --> 01:11:05.660
Have you seen since the book came out

1555
01:11:05.660 --> 01:11:10.010
and you've been speaking regularly on this,

1556
01:11:10.010 --> 01:11:14.540
have you seen changes, positive movement in this regard?

1557
01:11:14.540 --> 01:11:17.250
A little, but not, not too much.

1558
01:11:17.250 --> 01:11:18.120
Unfortunately.

1559
01:11:18.120 --> 01:11:21.060
I think there are some medical programs around this country

1560
01:11:21.060 --> 01:11:23.170
at least that are doing better than others.

1561
01:11:23.170 --> 01:11:25.730
There's, quite a variability.

1562
01:11:25.730 --> 01:11:27.830
So some are prioritizing it

1563
01:11:27.830 --> 01:11:29.490
and understanding its importance.

1564
01:11:29.490 --> 01:11:32.200
But overall no, I think that that same sort of

1565
01:11:32.200 --> 01:11:35.053
some of that hubris is still present.

1566
01:11:36.700 --> 01:11:38.120
What do we do to change that?

1567
01:11:38.120 --> 01:11:42.010
Well, you know, I tried early on speaking about this

1568
01:11:42.010 --> 01:11:45.250
from the statistics of the patients,

1569
01:11:45.250 --> 01:11:48.530
from a point of view of compassion and empathy

1570
01:11:48.530 --> 01:11:50.780
for our young residents.

1571
01:11:50.780 --> 01:11:54.430
And that sort of just falls on deaf ears.

1572
01:11:54.430 --> 01:11:57.210
What I realized is that you have to speak in a currency

1573
01:11:57.210 --> 01:11:58.720
with which, you know,

1574
01:11:58.720 --> 01:12:01.790
medical institutions and administrators will listen to,

1575
01:12:01.790 --> 01:12:04.110
which is dollars and cents.

1576
01:12:04.110 --> 01:12:06.030
And when you start to rack up the numbers

1577
01:12:06.030 --> 01:12:08.560
regarding malpractice suits caused by insufficient sleep--

1578
01:12:08.560 --> 01:12:09.970
Yeah, they tart paying attention.

1579
01:12:09.970 --> 01:12:11.213
They start paying attention.

1580
01:12:11.213 --> 01:12:12.812
So I was just stupid.

1581
01:12:12.812 --> 01:12:16.622
I thought about appealing in the wrong language.

1582
01:12:16.622 --> 01:12:18.210
You gotta follow the money.

1583
01:12:18.210 --> 01:12:19.898
Yeah, I know, yeah.

1584
01:12:19.898 --> 01:12:20.731
(both giggling)

1585
01:12:20.731 --> 01:12:24.023
But this, Halsted reminds me of,

1586
01:12:25.120 --> 01:12:26.990
that TV show, "The Knick".

1587
01:12:26.990 --> 01:12:28.590
Did you watch the show?

1588
01:12:28.590 --> 01:12:32.860
Steven Soderbergh did a limited series several years ago.

1589
01:12:32.860 --> 01:12:36.480
I think it was for Showtime, I can't remember.

1590
01:12:36.480 --> 01:12:40.790
But it's about this surgeon played by Clive Owen

1591
01:12:40.790 --> 01:12:43.750
at the turn of the century, it's literally 1900.

1592
01:12:43.750 --> 01:12:45.610
And he is,

1593
01:12:45.610 --> 01:12:49.020
he's like an opium addict and then he gets introduced

1594
01:12:49.020 --> 01:12:50.850
to cocaine and heroin, but he's,

1595
01:12:50.850 --> 01:12:53.560
he's this talented surgeon and he goes into the theater

1596
01:12:53.560 --> 01:12:55.880
and you see the crazy surgeries that they were doing

1597
01:12:55.880 --> 01:12:56.713
at the time.

1598
01:12:56.713 --> 01:12:58.360
It's quite an interesting document,

1599
01:12:58.360 --> 01:13:00.793
I think you would really like it, but I couldn't but think,

1600
01:13:00.793 --> 01:13:03.560
I wonder if this character is modeled after--

1601
01:13:03.560 --> 01:13:04.570
I suspect so.

1602
01:13:04.570 --> 01:13:06.840
I suspect if, you know, drug abuse, cocaine

1603
01:13:06.840 --> 01:13:10.400
and such was involved and he was this extraordinary power,

1604
01:13:10.400 --> 01:13:12.754
force that sort of birth into being, you know,

1605
01:13:12.754 --> 01:13:14.730
this new way of medical practice.

1606
01:13:14.730 --> 01:13:16.100
I, well, imagine it was based on--

1607
01:13:16.100 --> 01:13:17.470
Right, right, right.

1608
01:13:17.470 --> 01:13:21.877
Well, the relationship between substance and substance abuse

1609
01:13:21.877 --> 01:13:24.910
and addiction and sleep deprivation,

1610
01:13:24.910 --> 01:13:28.190
is another way in on this, you know, in this terrain.

1611
01:13:28.190 --> 01:13:29.770
I mean, when you're,

1612
01:13:29.770 --> 01:13:31.660
when you have all these doctors who are being compelled

1613
01:13:31.660 --> 01:13:32.530
to stay up so late,

1614
01:13:32.530 --> 01:13:36.620
they're gonna find exogenous ways of enhancing their ability

1615
01:13:36.620 --> 01:13:37.790
to do their jobs.

1616
01:13:37.790 --> 01:13:40.590
And the treatment center that I went to,

1617
01:13:40.590 --> 01:13:41.940
many years ago,

1618
01:13:41.940 --> 01:13:44.532
was known for having lots of doctors,

1619
01:13:44.532 --> 01:13:49.270
doctors and pilots, like the two people that you completely,

1620
01:13:49.270 --> 01:13:52.750
sort of divest yourself of any, of all control over to.

1621
01:13:52.750 --> 01:13:57.040
And I just remember being struck at how many surgeons

1622
01:13:57.040 --> 01:14:00.120
and pilots were in this treatment center, right?

1623
01:14:00.120 --> 01:14:03.540
Like there was a brain surgeon who was a morphine addict

1624
01:14:03.540 --> 01:14:05.710
and there was all kinds of pilots

1625
01:14:05.710 --> 01:14:06.870
that were addicted to Vicodin.

1626
01:14:06.870 --> 01:14:11.150
There were anesthesiologists that were fentanyl addicts,

1627
01:14:11.150 --> 01:14:14.280
they would steal it when they would not use the entire vial.

1628
01:14:14.280 --> 01:14:16.230
And they were using them,

1629
01:14:16.230 --> 01:14:19.482
primarily so that they could function in their job capacity.

1630
01:14:19.482 --> 01:14:21.010
That's right, yeah.
That's how it starts.

1631
01:14:21.010 --> 01:14:23.160
To sort of try to come, you know,

1632
01:14:23.160 --> 01:14:25.030
back from the disruption.

1633
01:14:25.030 --> 01:14:26.793
But what we know is that the,

1634
01:14:27.680 --> 01:14:30.081
the risk or the probability

1635
01:14:30.081 --> 01:14:33.170
of someone abusing drugs of abuse

1636
01:14:34.240 --> 01:14:36.390
in the medical profession is significantly higher

1637
01:14:36.390 --> 01:14:37.906
than the general population.

1638
01:14:37.906 --> 01:14:41.270
To me, a more striking and, well,

1639
01:14:41.270 --> 01:14:45.370
an equally striking and equally worrisome statistic recently

1640
01:14:45.370 --> 01:14:48.290
is that the rates of suicide,

1641
01:14:48.290 --> 01:14:51.900
are far higher in young residents.

1642
01:14:51.900 --> 01:14:54.840
And what we've discovered over the past,

1643
01:14:54.840 --> 01:14:57.330
maybe 10 years, and we've been doing a lot of this work too,

1644
01:14:57.330 --> 01:14:59.057
is the intimate relationship between sleep

1645
01:14:59.057 --> 01:15:01.090
and your mental health.

1646
01:15:01.090 --> 01:15:04.130
And, what we know is that when you are not getting

1647
01:15:04.130 --> 01:15:05.410
sufficient sleep,

1648
01:15:05.410 --> 01:15:10.270
your suicidal thoughts increase,

1649
01:15:10.270 --> 01:15:14.360
suicidal planning increases, suicide attempts increase,

1650
01:15:14.360 --> 01:15:19.360
and tragically suicide completion also increases as well.

1651
01:15:19.510 --> 01:15:21.440
And more generally what we've discovered,

1652
01:15:21.440 --> 01:15:23.850
and I've been doing this for about 20 years now,

1653
01:15:23.850 --> 01:15:25.500
we have not been able to discover

1654
01:15:25.500 --> 01:15:30.500
a single psychiatric condition in which sleep is normal.

1655
01:15:30.630 --> 01:15:33.560
And so I think sleep has a profound story to tell,

1656
01:15:33.560 --> 01:15:35.900
and our understanding, our treatment,

1657
01:15:35.900 --> 01:15:39.203
maybe even our prevention of grave mental illness.

1658
01:15:40.170 --> 01:15:41.870
Well, certainly in saying that

1659
01:15:41.870 --> 01:15:43.800
it should be the first stop

1660
01:15:43.800 --> 01:15:46.050
on the kind of treatment protocol, right?

1661
01:15:46.050 --> 01:15:47.100
Like how is your sleep?

1662
01:15:47.100 --> 01:15:48.220
Let's deal with that first,

1663
01:15:48.220 --> 01:15:50.670
before we look at pharmaceutical interventions.

1664
01:15:50.670 --> 01:15:52.530
Yeah, it's a stabilizing force

1665
01:15:52.530 --> 01:15:54.450
and we know it's a stabilizing force,

1666
01:15:54.450 --> 01:15:56.720
both in terms of your psychology

1667
01:15:56.720 --> 01:15:58.600
as we mentioned impulsivity,

1668
01:15:58.600 --> 01:16:02.310
but also just from a basic reward brain sensitivity

1669
01:16:02.310 --> 01:16:04.440
that your addiction potential

1670
01:16:04.440 --> 01:16:08.670
from a brain perspective is higher with insufficient sleep.

1671
01:16:08.670 --> 01:16:12.020
Same individual, two different sleep conditions,

1672
01:16:12.020 --> 01:16:14.990
two different addictive profiles.

1673
01:16:14.990 --> 01:16:18.703
Wow. I have to reassess my whole life now.

1674
01:16:18.703 --> 01:16:22.370
Oh, I'm so sorry-
No, it's fine.

1675
01:16:22.370 --> 01:16:23.203
No, it's good.

1676
01:16:23.203 --> 01:16:26.600
I wanna talk about sleep in adolescents and teens,

1677
01:16:26.600 --> 01:16:28.120
'cause I think the work that you're doing here

1678
01:16:28.120 --> 01:16:30.080
and the issues that you're trying to, you know,

1679
01:16:30.080 --> 01:16:33.030
raise awareness around are super important.

1680
01:16:33.030 --> 01:16:36.320
The implications of chronic sleep deprivation

1681
01:16:36.320 --> 01:16:39.580
in young people, the relationship to, you know,

1682
01:16:39.580 --> 01:16:42.570
early school hours and how this plays out

1683
01:16:42.570 --> 01:16:44.850
in terms of academic potential

1684
01:16:44.850 --> 01:16:47.750
and the mental health implications that you just mentioned.

1685
01:16:47.750 --> 01:16:51.330
Yeah, so there's been a remarkable amount of work,

1686
01:16:51.330 --> 01:16:54.290
looking at this issue of early school start times,

1687
01:16:54.290 --> 01:16:59.290
this incessant model of marching back the school clock hours

1688
01:16:59.670 --> 01:17:01.990
and the summary of the evidence, really

1689
01:17:01.990 --> 01:17:03.610
I think it goes the following.

1690
01:17:03.610 --> 01:17:06.800
Firstly, we see that academic,

1691
01:17:06.800 --> 01:17:09.550
when you shift schools to a later school start time.

1692
01:17:09.550 --> 01:17:12.470
So when you do a causal intervention, what happens?

1693
01:17:12.470 --> 01:17:15.414
Firstly the academic grades increase,

1694
01:17:15.414 --> 01:17:17.950
the truancy rates decrease,

1695
01:17:17.950 --> 01:17:22.950
psychological and psychiatric referrals also decrease.

1696
01:17:23.110 --> 01:17:26.760
But what we also discovered is that the life expectancy

1697
01:17:26.760 --> 01:17:28.670
of those students increased.

1698
01:17:28.670 --> 01:17:30.830
And you may think, well, hang on a second,

1699
01:17:30.830 --> 01:17:33.380
how does that work?

1700
01:17:33.380 --> 01:17:35.800
Well, the leading cause of death

1701
01:17:35.800 --> 01:17:39.710
in late stage adolescent teens is actually not suicide,

1702
01:17:39.710 --> 01:17:41.350
it's road traffic accidents,

1703
01:17:41.350 --> 01:17:44.120
and here sleep matters enormously.

1704
01:17:44.120 --> 01:17:45.420
And there was one good example,

1705
01:17:45.420 --> 01:17:48.820
I think it came from Teton County in Wyoming.

1706
01:17:48.820 --> 01:17:50.370
They shifted their school start times

1707
01:17:50.370 --> 01:17:52.830
from around 07:30 in the morning

1708
01:17:52.830 --> 01:17:54.980
to just before nine o'clock in the morning.

1709
01:17:55.980 --> 01:17:58.833
The only thing more remarkable than the extra hour of sleep

1710
01:17:58.833 --> 01:18:01.100
that those kids reported getting,

1711
01:18:01.100 --> 01:18:03.800
was the drop in road traffic accidents.

1712
01:18:03.800 --> 01:18:06.370
That following year after the time switch,

1713
01:18:06.370 --> 01:18:09.760
there was a 70%, seven zero reduction

1714
01:18:09.760 --> 01:18:13.210
in car crashes in students, 16 to 18.

1715
01:18:13.210 --> 01:18:14.830
Statistically unbelievable.

1716
01:18:14.830 --> 01:18:15.710
It blows my mind.

1717
01:18:15.710 --> 01:18:16.910
Now, you know, statistically,

1718
01:18:16.910 --> 01:18:19.790
you can give a relevance to that.

1719
01:18:19.790 --> 01:18:22.820
Think of the advent of ABS systems in cars,

1720
01:18:22.820 --> 01:18:23.860
anti-lock brake systems

1721
01:18:23.860 --> 01:18:26.960
that stop your wheels from locking up into heartbreaking.

1722
01:18:26.960 --> 01:18:30.610
That dropped accident rates by around 20 to 25%

1723
01:18:30.610 --> 01:18:32.730
and it was deemed a revolution.

1724
01:18:32.730 --> 01:18:35.910
Yet here is a simple biological factor,

1725
01:18:35.910 --> 01:18:38.040
giving our kids the sleep that they need

1726
01:18:38.040 --> 01:18:41.470
that will drop accident rates by up to 70%.

1727
01:18:41.470 --> 01:18:46.330
So if our goal as educators truly is to educate

1728
01:18:46.330 --> 01:18:48.590
and not risk lives in the process,

1729
01:18:48.590 --> 01:18:50.400
then we are failing our children

1730
01:18:50.400 --> 01:18:52.570
in the most spectacular manner

1731
01:18:52.570 --> 01:18:55.463
with this incessant model of early school start times.

1732
01:18:56.540 --> 01:18:59.700
When sleep is abundant, minds flourish,

1733
01:18:59.700 --> 01:19:01.562
when it's not, they don't.

1734
01:19:01.562 --> 01:19:03.040
There's so much education

1735
01:19:03.040 --> 01:19:06.730
I think that that needs to be done in this area.

1736
01:19:06.730 --> 01:19:11.730
As a parent of two teens, I've got four kids, two are boys.

1737
01:19:12.090 --> 01:19:15.880
Any parent knows how difficult it is to wake up

1738
01:19:15.880 --> 01:19:17.197
a young adolescent in the morning

1739
01:19:17.197 --> 01:19:19.720
and the frustration that comes with that.

1740
01:19:19.720 --> 01:19:22.440
But what I've come to better understand

1741
01:19:22.440 --> 01:19:24.640
by dent of your work,

1742
01:19:24.640 --> 01:19:29.540
is how crucial sleep is for that developing brain.

1743
01:19:29.540 --> 01:19:32.680
And during COVID we have one child,

1744
01:19:32.680 --> 01:19:35.400
our younger child who's being homeschooled right now.

1745
01:19:35.400 --> 01:19:38.460
We've let her sleep in as long as she wants

1746
01:19:38.460 --> 01:19:43.110
and we don't start her first class until noon.

1747
01:19:43.110 --> 01:19:45.220
And so she's getting a ton of sleep,

1748
01:19:45.220 --> 01:19:48.250
but if you even tried to wake her up at nine o'clock,

1749
01:19:48.250 --> 01:19:49.430
I mean, forget it,

1750
01:19:49.430 --> 01:19:51.330
you're gonna spend the next hour and a half

1751
01:19:51.330 --> 01:19:53.290
trying to get this kid up.

1752
01:19:53.290 --> 01:19:57.010
So clearly there's a reason why that state of sleep

1753
01:19:57.010 --> 01:19:58.290
is so deep, right?

1754
01:19:58.290 --> 01:20:01.870
Like that, it is crucial in this developing, you know,

1755
01:20:01.870 --> 01:20:03.660
in this developing mind.

1756
01:20:03.660 --> 01:20:07.800
Yeah, and you know, we often have that classic idea of,

1757
01:20:07.800 --> 01:20:10.950
you know, a parent at the weekend, pulling the sheets off,

1758
01:20:10.950 --> 01:20:12.739
you know, the teenagers ripping open the curtains--

1759
01:20:12.739 --> 01:20:13.880
The trauma of wasting the--
And saying, you know,

1760
01:20:13.880 --> 01:20:15.403
you're wasting the day.

1761
01:20:17.070 --> 01:20:20.360
But firstly, what we know is that it's not their fault,

1762
01:20:20.360 --> 01:20:22.980
because during that adolescent transition,

1763
01:20:22.980 --> 01:20:26.270
there is a biological shift in their 24-hour rhythm

1764
01:20:26.270 --> 01:20:30.860
that they now want to go to bed later and wake up later.

1765
01:20:30.860 --> 01:20:34.260
So asking a teenager to wake up at seven o'clock

1766
01:20:34.260 --> 01:20:37.760
in the morning and operate and conduct themselves

1767
01:20:37.760 --> 01:20:41.060
with good grace and be able to learn effectively

1768
01:20:41.060 --> 01:20:43.210
is like (indistinct) an adults to, you know,

1769
01:20:43.210 --> 01:20:45.850
to wake up at 03:30 or 04:00 in the morning

1770
01:20:45.850 --> 01:20:47.903
and be the best version of yourself.

1771
01:20:47.903 --> 01:20:51.200
If I was to wake up at three o'clock in the morning

1772
01:20:51.200 --> 01:20:53.330
and come through to the kitchen and oh, we both wake,

1773
01:20:53.330 --> 01:20:55.110
I'd have to say to my partner, you know,

1774
01:20:55.110 --> 01:20:56.590
I would say, "Look darling."

1775
01:20:56.590 --> 01:20:57.727
And she would say like,

1776
01:20:57.727 --> 01:20:59.560
"Why are you so a moody?"

1777
01:20:59.560 --> 01:21:01.080
I'm just not the best version of myself

1778
01:21:01.080 --> 01:21:03.449
'cause I've woken up too early

1779
01:21:03.449 --> 01:21:04.950
and she will definitely tell you that.

1780
01:21:04.950 --> 01:21:08.510
But I think it's, it's the same, you know,

1781
01:21:08.510 --> 01:21:11.310
misgiving because we don't understand how sleep works

1782
01:21:11.310 --> 01:21:13.330
with our teenagers.

1783
01:21:13.330 --> 01:21:16.410
And so putting them in the school at that time

1784
01:21:16.410 --> 01:21:19.410
seven or eight in the morning essentially is, you know,

1785
01:21:19.410 --> 01:21:21.040
educating them amnesic.

1786
01:21:21.040 --> 01:21:24.610
at that stage they are leaky sieves and what will go in,

1787
01:21:24.610 --> 01:21:27.030
will just come out the other side.

1788
01:21:27.030 --> 01:21:28.820
So that's the first thing that's happening.

1789
01:21:28.820 --> 01:21:31.550
It's not their fault, it's just their biology.

1790
01:21:31.550 --> 01:21:33.903
And then second at the weekend,

1791
01:21:33.903 --> 01:21:37.050
they're trying to sleep off a debt that we've lumbered them

1792
01:21:37.050 --> 01:21:38.000
with during the week

1793
01:21:38.000 --> 01:21:40.900
because of these early school start times.

1794
01:21:40.900 --> 01:21:43.110
And if you ask parents,

1795
01:21:43.110 --> 01:21:47.520
if you sort of question parents of teenagers,

1796
01:21:47.520 --> 01:21:50.760
what proportion of parents think that their teen

1797
01:21:50.760 --> 01:21:54.010
is getting sufficient sleep and more than 70% of them say,

1798
01:21:54.010 --> 01:21:55.490
I think my teen is doing fine.

1799
01:21:55.490 --> 01:21:56.970
I think they're getting the sleep of need.

1800
01:21:56.970 --> 01:22:00.900
When in reality, less than 15% of those teens

1801
01:22:00.900 --> 01:22:03.690
are actually getting the sleep that they need.

1802
01:22:03.690 --> 01:22:07.250
So there is a parent to child mismatch

1803
01:22:07.250 --> 01:22:08.790
in sleep understanding.

1804
01:22:08.790 --> 01:22:11.870
And as a consequence, there is a parent to child

1805
01:22:11.870 --> 01:22:14.240
transmission of sleep neglect.

1806
01:22:14.240 --> 01:22:16.970
Parents don't see it. They don't understand it.

1807
01:22:16.970 --> 01:22:21.900
And so, you know, in 15 or you know, 10 or 20 years time

1808
01:22:21.900 --> 01:22:25.940
that teenager lo and behold seems to have amnesia

1809
01:22:25.940 --> 01:22:28.460
and they, with their own kids will do the same thing.

1810
01:22:28.460 --> 01:22:29.550
They'll pull the sheets off and say,

1811
01:22:29.550 --> 01:22:32.250
you're wasting the day because it was taught to them

1812
01:22:32.250 --> 01:22:36.130
by their parents that sleep and getting the right timing

1813
01:22:36.130 --> 01:22:37.920
of sleep and the right amount of sleep

1814
01:22:37.920 --> 01:22:39.750
is something shameful.

1815
01:22:39.750 --> 01:22:44.603
So for that young person, do they need more hours?

1816
01:22:45.480 --> 01:22:47.086
They do, so--

1817
01:22:47.086 --> 01:22:49.090
The eight hour rule doesn't really apply.

1818
01:22:49.090 --> 01:22:50.540
No that's for adults.

1819
01:22:50.540 --> 01:22:54.320
And in fact, the brain doesn't stop developing

1820
01:22:54.320 --> 01:22:57.540
until it's about 25 years old

1821
01:22:57.540 --> 01:23:00.290
and sleep plays a critical role

1822
01:23:00.290 --> 01:23:02.060
in what we call brain plasticity,

1823
01:23:02.060 --> 01:23:04.090
which is modeling the brain.

1824
01:23:04.090 --> 01:23:07.473
It plays a role early in life in the first couple of years,

1825
01:23:08.590 --> 01:23:12.040
sleep seems to actually help wire up the brain.

1826
01:23:12.040 --> 01:23:15.260
So it's almost like sleep at that time of life comes in

1827
01:23:15.260 --> 01:23:19.530
to a new neighborhood and like an internet service provider,

1828
01:23:19.530 --> 01:23:21.350
it wires all of those homes

1829
01:23:21.350 --> 01:23:24.090
with high-speed fiber optic cables.

1830
01:23:24.090 --> 01:23:29.060
But then later in life, that's when during the teenage years

1831
01:23:29.060 --> 01:23:30.680
we've actually realized

1832
01:23:30.680 --> 01:23:33.990
which homes are using the high bandwidth

1833
01:23:33.990 --> 01:23:36.700
and which homes aren't really sort of drawing

1834
01:23:36.700 --> 01:23:39.593
on that sort of, you know, that broad band speed.

1835
01:23:40.460 --> 01:23:44.050
And so then the role of the brain, it goes from expanding,

1836
01:23:44.050 --> 01:23:48.060
creating lots of connections, which is called neurogenesis

1837
01:23:48.060 --> 01:23:50.640
to then actually synaptic pruning,

1838
01:23:50.640 --> 01:23:53.360
which is where we now have to make the brain efficient

1839
01:23:53.360 --> 01:23:54.550
for adulthood.

1840
01:23:54.550 --> 01:23:58.980
We sculpt away the unnecessary and we keep the necessary

1841
01:23:58.980 --> 01:24:03.563
and sleep performs that role too in later teenage years.

1842
01:24:04.430 --> 01:24:07.100
And as a consequence,

1843
01:24:07.100 --> 01:24:11.540
if you are chronically under sleeping a teenager,

1844
01:24:11.540 --> 01:24:14.880
you're effectively stunting brain development,

1845
01:24:14.880 --> 01:24:17.290
and no one would wish that for that child.

1846
01:24:17.290 --> 01:24:19.970
Right, so you impede neurogenesis.

1847
01:24:19.970 --> 01:24:22.820
So there's fewer pathways to choose from.

1848
01:24:22.820 --> 01:24:27.440
And then as you age, then you're selecting,

1849
01:24:27.440 --> 01:24:29.480
you're basically, you have less to choose from, right?

1850
01:24:29.480 --> 01:24:32.110
So you're just limiting the CA the brain's capacity

1851
01:24:32.110 --> 01:24:33.250
to do its job well.

1852
01:24:33.250 --> 01:24:35.900
So early in life in the first five or so years,

1853
01:24:35.900 --> 01:24:38.030
you want to sort of, you know,

1854
01:24:38.030 --> 01:24:39.010
it's almost spray and pray attitude

1855
01:24:39.010 --> 01:24:40.910
a little bit like in investing,

1856
01:24:40.910 --> 01:24:43.370
you just, you know, gift all regions of the brain

1857
01:24:43.370 --> 01:24:44.480
lots of connections.

1858
01:24:44.480 --> 01:24:49.360
And then you let experience and time teach the brain

1859
01:24:49.360 --> 01:24:53.870
which highways and sort of trippy trees and avenues

1860
01:24:53.870 --> 01:24:56.270
and lanes would you like to keep

1861
01:24:56.270 --> 01:24:57.400
and which should be removed.

1862
01:24:57.400 --> 01:24:59.790
So now, as you shift later in life,

1863
01:24:59.790 --> 01:25:03.320
it's all about efficiency. Let's get this brain efficient.

1864
01:25:03.320 --> 01:25:04.200
'Cause right now,

1865
01:25:04.200 --> 01:25:06.700
when you've got this too much connectivity,

1866
01:25:06.700 --> 01:25:10.870
it's good to sort of as a general blueprint to lay down,

1867
01:25:10.870 --> 01:25:15.870
but then let life tell you which of those sort of networks

1868
01:25:15.880 --> 01:25:19.380
you should enhance and which you should actually remove.

1869
01:25:19.380 --> 01:25:22.690
And if later in life that we're removing those.

1870
01:25:22.690 --> 01:25:25.724
And as I said, it's not until we're about 25,

1871
01:25:25.724 --> 01:25:27.950
that the brain has finished all of that maturation.

1872
01:25:27.950 --> 01:25:30.210
And that's why the recommendation

1873
01:25:30.210 --> 01:25:32.280
is seven to nine hours in adults.

1874
01:25:32.280 --> 01:25:35.876
And I would argue in adults once they're past 25 years old,

1875
01:25:35.876 --> 01:25:39.950
before that you'd need much more sleep.

1876
01:25:39.950 --> 01:25:42.480
So what would be the ideal amount of sleep

1877
01:25:42.480 --> 01:25:44.940
for the adolescent or young teen?

1878
01:25:44.940 --> 01:25:48.860
Yeah, it's usually somewhere between eight to 10 hours

1879
01:25:48.860 --> 01:25:52.410
and going up to maybe 11 hours depending on the teen.

1880
01:25:52.410 --> 01:25:53.810
And if people wanna find out,

1881
01:25:53.810 --> 01:25:55.700
there's a great informational sheet,

1882
01:25:55.700 --> 01:25:59.140
they can just go to the national sleep foundation.

1883
01:25:59.140 --> 01:26:01.670
It's the nationalsleepfoundation.org.

1884
01:26:01.670 --> 01:26:04.090
And you can look at the current guidelines

1885
01:26:04.090 --> 01:26:07.000
that we and other scientists have sort of tried to

1886
01:26:07.000 --> 01:26:09.040
gather all of the information say, look,

1887
01:26:09.040 --> 01:26:12.740
where should we position these windows of sleep duration

1888
01:26:12.740 --> 01:26:14.840
based on different age ranges.

1889
01:26:14.840 --> 01:26:17.465
So it's a great resource if people are thinking about that

1890
01:26:17.465 --> 01:26:19.560
for their kids and for themselves as well.

1891
01:26:19.560 --> 01:26:23.460
If you could bend the ear of the secretary of education,

1892
01:26:23.460 --> 01:26:25.200
I mean, what would be your recommendation

1893
01:26:25.200 --> 01:26:28.480
in terms of school start time, like 09:00 a.m.?

1894
01:26:28.480 --> 01:26:30.537
I think for teenagers, I would say 10:00 a.m.

1895
01:26:30.537 --> 01:26:32.400
10:00.
10:00 the minimum.

1896
01:26:32.400 --> 01:26:35.210
And what's interesting is that the younger kids,

1897
01:26:35.210 --> 01:26:36.710
they can actually wake up earlier

1898
01:26:36.710 --> 01:26:38.760
'cause they haven't gone through that adolescent transition.

1899
01:26:38.760 --> 01:26:41.313
So, they probably could be going to school

1900
01:26:41.313 --> 01:26:44.390
and learning effectively at let's say 09:00 a.m.

1901
01:26:44.390 --> 01:26:46.730
or even 08:30 a.m.

1902
01:26:46.730 --> 01:26:50.374
But in those studies, they carefully asked the question.

1903
01:26:50.374 --> 01:26:54.980
We can't set different times for different age brackets.

1904
01:26:54.980 --> 01:26:57.000
You know, that's just not practical.

1905
01:26:57.000 --> 01:26:59.240
And I understand

1906
01:26:59.240 --> 01:27:02.600
why there is resistance in the school system.

1907
01:27:02.600 --> 01:27:05.120
You can say, why aren't we starting that early?

1908
01:27:05.120 --> 01:27:07.830
Some of it has to do a little bit with the bus unions,

1909
01:27:07.830 --> 01:27:10.000
the bus networks that tried to get kids to school.

1910
01:27:10.000 --> 01:27:10.833
And I get that.

1911
01:27:10.833 --> 01:27:14.900
I get, you know, it's not an easy problem to solve,

1912
01:27:14.900 --> 01:27:17.550
but I also realized that we've put people on the moon

1913
01:27:18.533 --> 01:27:19.510
and I think we can solve--

1914
01:27:19.510 --> 01:27:21.974
Even tougher problems to solve.

1915
01:27:21.974 --> 01:27:23.340
And so I'm not trivializing it.

1916
01:27:23.340 --> 01:27:25.960
It's a big problem and I don't have all of the answers.

1917
01:27:25.960 --> 01:27:27.130
I'm just the scientist.

1918
01:27:27.130 --> 01:27:30.790
Well, there's the kind of mechanistic logistical aspect

1919
01:27:30.790 --> 01:27:31.750
of solving that problem.

1920
01:27:31.750 --> 01:27:34.350
But there's also the mental hurdle

1921
01:27:34.350 --> 01:27:36.000
which comes with education, right?

1922
01:27:36.000 --> 01:27:38.580
Like getting somebody's head wrapped around the idea

1923
01:27:38.580 --> 01:27:40.370
of why a 10:00 a.m. school start

1924
01:27:40.370 --> 01:27:44.620
is in the best interest of the child is a hurdle.

1925
01:27:44.620 --> 01:27:48.425
And you know, for most parents, that's the genetic legacy.

1926
01:27:48.425 --> 01:27:50.537
That's what you--
They're like,

1927
01:27:50.537 --> 01:27:51.370
I gotta get to work too.

1928
01:27:51.370 --> 01:27:52.440
Like, what am I supposed to, you know, so.

1929
01:27:52.440 --> 01:27:54.090
And, and that's the hard part.

1930
01:27:54.090 --> 01:27:56.130
So what they did is they looked at say,

1931
01:27:56.130 --> 01:27:59.450
okay, let's say that we started school at 10 o'clock,

1932
01:27:59.450 --> 01:28:01.670
that's only gonna help the teenagers,

1933
01:28:01.670 --> 01:28:05.170
but would it hurt the kids who are younger

1934
01:28:05.170 --> 01:28:06.760
because that would then say, well,

1935
01:28:06.760 --> 01:28:09.640
we can't do this one time fits all phenomenon.

1936
01:28:09.640 --> 01:28:12.240
And what they found is that it didn't hurt the younger kids.

1937
01:28:12.240 --> 01:28:14.350
They were learning just as effectively,

1938
01:28:14.350 --> 01:28:15.480
but in the teenagers,

1939
01:28:15.480 --> 01:28:17.180
they were learning far more effectively.

1940
01:28:17.180 --> 01:28:20.380
In fact, if you look across the day and you ask,

1941
01:28:20.380 --> 01:28:22.080
where are those school start times

1942
01:28:22.080 --> 01:28:24.000
really hurting the teenagers,

1943
01:28:24.000 --> 01:28:25.970
it's in the first half of the day,

1944
01:28:25.970 --> 01:28:27.280
because they're mostly asleep

1945
01:28:27.280 --> 01:28:30.058
and you ask any high school teacher,

1946
01:28:30.058 --> 01:28:35.058
what does my class look like first thing in the morning

1947
01:28:35.090 --> 01:28:36.700
versus in the afternoon?

1948
01:28:36.700 --> 01:28:39.483
They are usually very different as an audience.

1949
01:28:41.490 --> 01:28:42.343
What about,

1950
01:28:45.190 --> 01:28:50.190
dealing with things like SSRIs and,

1951
01:28:52.150 --> 01:28:56.480
and treating ADHD with Adderall and Ritalin

1952
01:28:56.480 --> 01:28:59.650
and, you know, the overmedication of our young people

1953
01:28:59.650 --> 01:29:02.570
and the implication on sleep and brain development.

1954
01:29:02.570 --> 01:29:05.253
Yeah, I wrote a little bit about this in the book.

1955
01:29:06.090 --> 01:29:09.700
It is unfortunate that if you look at ADHD,

1956
01:29:09.700 --> 01:29:12.430
the current recommended treatment are things like Adderall

1957
01:29:12.430 --> 01:29:14.500
and Ritalin.

1958
01:29:14.500 --> 01:29:17.740
And they are some versions of something

1959
01:29:17.740 --> 01:29:19.883
that looks very much like amphetamine.

1960
01:29:21.460 --> 01:29:24.810
And I'm not dismissing the idea that ADHD exists.

1961
01:29:24.810 --> 01:29:27.390
I'm not one of those people, it clearly does.

1962
01:29:27.390 --> 01:29:31.650
I also am not anti-medication by any means.

1963
01:29:31.650 --> 01:29:33.370
But what I would say is that,

1964
01:29:33.370 --> 01:29:35.850
unfortunately, those medications

1965
01:29:35.850 --> 01:29:39.570
are very strong, wake promoting medications.

1966
01:29:39.570 --> 01:29:42.380
I mean, if you ask an amphetamine addict, you know,

1967
01:29:42.380 --> 01:29:45.240
if they need sleep, when they using amphetamines,

1968
01:29:45.240 --> 01:29:46.460
they will laugh at you, right?

1969
01:29:46.460 --> 01:29:48.610
They'll say, are you kidding me?

1970
01:29:48.610 --> 01:29:50.890
I can go straight without sleep.

1971
01:29:50.890 --> 01:29:54.390
And so maybe we need to think differently

1972
01:29:54.390 --> 01:29:58.110
about at least the timing of that medication.

1973
01:29:58.110 --> 01:30:00.450
Do we wanna be medicating kids in the morning

1974
01:30:00.450 --> 01:30:01.790
or in the evening?

1975
01:30:01.790 --> 01:30:03.720
Because if those things are wake promoting,

1976
01:30:03.720 --> 01:30:07.530
we know that kids with ADHD have non-normal sleep,

1977
01:30:07.530 --> 01:30:09.340
they actually have disrupted sleep.

1978
01:30:09.340 --> 01:30:12.540
And we're also starting to find that those medications

1979
01:30:12.540 --> 01:30:15.610
themselves are sleep disruptors.

1980
01:30:15.610 --> 01:30:19.590
And so one wonders what would be the efficacy,

1981
01:30:19.590 --> 01:30:22.870
the benefit of those medications, which there are,

1982
01:30:22.870 --> 01:30:25.520
but just timed correctly.

1983
01:30:25.520 --> 01:30:28.410
And you are at least able to try and protect sleep.

1984
01:30:28.410 --> 01:30:30.810
Or what if we could just come up with better medications

1985
01:30:30.810 --> 01:30:34.000
that aren't so destructive to sleep?

1986
01:30:34.000 --> 01:30:36.160
I think that to me would be a better approach.

1987
01:30:36.160 --> 01:30:41.160
Is there research on antidepressant medication

1988
01:30:42.150 --> 01:30:45.280
like the SSRIs and the implications for sleep?

1989
01:30:45.280 --> 01:30:46.113
There are, yeah.

1990
01:30:46.113 --> 01:30:48.260
So sleep is usually not normative

1991
01:30:48.260 --> 01:30:49.750
when you're taking antidepressants.

1992
01:30:49.750 --> 01:30:53.310
Now that's a general statement and it's too general in fact.

1993
01:30:53.310 --> 01:30:56.740
It really depends on which type of antidepressant,

1994
01:30:56.740 --> 01:30:58.330
because different antidepressants

1995
01:30:58.330 --> 01:31:02.000
will shift different neurochemical systems in the brain.

1996
01:31:02.000 --> 01:31:07.000
So SSRIs stands for Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitor.

1997
01:31:07.290 --> 01:31:09.910
And serotonin is a key neurochemical

1998
01:31:09.910 --> 01:31:12.120
in the regulation of sleep.

1999
01:31:12.120 --> 01:31:14.160
We have found historically

2000
01:31:14.160 --> 01:31:16.120
that people taking antidepressants

2001
01:31:16.120 --> 01:31:18.170
will typically have a reduction in REM sleep.

2002
01:31:18.170 --> 01:31:21.820
It really seems to hit hard on REM sleep.

2003
01:31:21.820 --> 01:31:25.707
And as we may come on to discuss sleep

2004
01:31:25.707 --> 01:31:27.500
and particularly REM sleep

2005
01:31:27.500 --> 01:31:30.623
provides a form of emotional first aid.

2006
01:31:31.500 --> 01:31:35.933
REM sleep is what I've described as overnight therapy.

2007
01:31:36.860 --> 01:31:39.540
And there's nothing that I've seen that, you know,

2008
01:31:39.540 --> 01:31:43.170
sort of is that much further from its effect size

2009
01:31:43.170 --> 01:31:44.850
based on the evidence.

2010
01:31:44.850 --> 01:31:47.260
And so one wonders whether or not

2011
01:31:47.260 --> 01:31:49.727
we can look to alternative medications

2012
01:31:49.727 --> 01:31:54.727
that again, have at least some conceptual sensitivity

2013
01:31:55.280 --> 01:31:57.170
to the things that regulate sleep

2014
01:31:57.170 --> 01:31:59.453
and try to stay away from disrupting sleep.

2015
01:32:00.300 --> 01:32:04.550
If sleep offers some form of assistance to depression,

2016
01:32:04.550 --> 01:32:08.620
and we know that insufficient sleep predicts depression,

2017
01:32:08.620 --> 01:32:12.590
we know that people who are depressed don't sleep very well,

2018
01:32:12.590 --> 01:32:14.703
they have abnormal sleep patterns.

2019
01:32:14.703 --> 01:32:19.180
So if sleep is a part of that disorder

2020
01:32:19.180 --> 01:32:22.360
and disordered sleep as a part of depression,

2021
01:32:22.360 --> 01:32:25.610
then shouldn't we think about the medications that they take

2022
01:32:25.610 --> 01:32:27.810
that are also sleep disruptive.

2023
01:32:27.810 --> 01:32:31.090
And maybe think about finding ways where we time again,

2024
01:32:31.090 --> 01:32:33.240
the onset of the medication.

2025
01:32:33.240 --> 01:32:36.410
I don't know how many doctors described to their patients,

2026
01:32:36.410 --> 01:32:39.540
not just take two of these, but when you know,

2027
01:32:39.540 --> 01:32:42.080
seven o'clock in the morning could be very different

2028
01:32:42.080 --> 01:32:43.070
than seven o'clock in the evening.

2029
01:32:43.070 --> 01:32:44.980
In fact, that's true of many medications

2030
01:32:44.980 --> 01:32:47.180
in terms of their sleep disruptive capacity.

2031
01:32:48.700 --> 01:32:52.960
For somebody who, for whatever reason schedule-wise,

2032
01:32:52.960 --> 01:32:55.660
or whatnot, can't seem to carve out

2033
01:32:55.660 --> 01:32:57.923
the eight hours of sleep a night,

2034
01:32:59.170 --> 01:33:03.450
can this be made up for through napping

2035
01:33:03.450 --> 01:33:05.950
or by phasic or polyphasic sleep

2036
01:33:05.950 --> 01:33:07.330
like there always seems to be people

2037
01:33:07.330 --> 01:33:08.830
who are doing all kinds of experiments

2038
01:33:08.830 --> 01:33:09.920
with polyphasic sleep.

2039
01:33:09.920 --> 01:33:11.640
But to my mind, I mean,

2040
01:33:11.640 --> 01:33:14.190
you know, obviously more way more than I do,

2041
01:33:14.190 --> 01:33:16.640
but it seems like those experiments always

2042
01:33:16.640 --> 01:33:18.042
like somebody who's like,

2043
01:33:18.042 --> 01:33:20.490
I'm just gonna sleep every two hours, every four hours,

2044
01:33:20.490 --> 01:33:22.720
but that never lasts very long.

2045
01:33:22.720 --> 01:33:23.730
They always end up going back.

2046
01:33:23.730 --> 01:33:25.560
No. It doesn't serve.

2047
01:33:25.560 --> 01:33:29.040
That sort of idea of what's called highly polyphasic sleep.

2048
01:33:29.040 --> 01:33:31.820
As some people describe it as the Uber man sort of schedule,

2049
01:33:31.820 --> 01:33:34.034
some people have said DaVinci once described it--

2050
01:33:34.034 --> 01:33:35.674
It's part of this like self optimization--

2051
01:33:35.674 --> 01:33:36.979
Biohacking.
Kind of thing

2052
01:33:36.979 --> 01:33:38.646
but it doesn't work.

2053
01:33:39.529 --> 01:33:41.333
Not good a idea.
No, it really doesn't.

2054
01:33:41.333 --> 01:33:44.800
And you know, there is nothing in our biology

2055
01:33:44.800 --> 01:33:47.630
